events costly repairs\n- Ensures consistent hot water supply\n- Maintains safe operation\n\n## Annual Maintenance Checklist\n\n### 1. Safety Inspection\n\n- Check pressure relief valve\n- Inspect anode rod\n- Test temperature settings\n- Examine gas line or electrical connections\n\n### 2. Tank Maintenance\n\n- Flush sediment from tank\n- Clean burner assembly\n- Check for leaks\n- Inspect heating elements\n\n## 3. Inspect and Replace the Anode Rod\n\nThe anode rod plays a crucial role in preventing corrosion inside your water heater tank. Over time,\nit deteriorates and requires replacement to keep the tank from rusting.\n\n- Check the anode rod every one to three years.\n- Replace it if more than 50% is worn or coated with calcium.\n- Use a socket wrench to remove the rod and install a new one as needed.\n- A healthy anode rod can significantly extend the life of your heater.\n\n## 4. Adjust the Thermostat\n\nMaintaining the right temperature setting helps avoid overheating, reduces energy consumption, and\nprevents scalding.\n\n- The recommended temperature is typically around 120°F (49°C).\n- Lowering the temperature can reduce energy bills and minimize mineral buildup.\n- Use a household thermometer to verify water temperature at the tap.\n\n## 5. Insulate the Heater and Pipes\n\nInsulating your water heater and the first few feet of hot and cold water pipes helps retain heat\nand improve efficiency.\n\n- Install a water heater blanket if your unit is in an unheated area.\n- Use pipe insulation sleeves to reduce heat loss and prevent freezing.\n- Make sure to avoid covering important components like the thermostat or burner area.\n\n## 6. Check for Leaks and Corrosion\n\nPeriodic visual inspections can help detect small issues before they become major failures.\n\n- Look for rust, corrosion, or pooling water around the base of the heater.\n- Check all visible pipes and connections for signs of leakage.\n- Tighten fittings or replace seals where necessary.\n\n## 7. Test the Pressure Relief Valve\n\nThe temperature and pressure relief valve (T\\&P valve) prevents excessive pressure buildup, which\ncan be dangerous.\n\n- Lift the valve handle slightly and let it snap back; you should hear a gurgling sound as some\n  water is released.\n- If the valve doesn’t release water or keeps leaking afterward, it should be replaced immediately.\n\nBy implementing these practical maintenance tasks, you ensure your water heater operates safely and\nefficiently for many years. A proactive approach not only protects your investment but also\nguarantees a steady supply of hot water when you need it most.\n\nIf you’re unsure about performing any of these tasks yourself, consider scheduling a professional\ninspection. Our specialists can help you maintain your system with precision and care, providing\npeace of mind and long-term savings. Let us assist you in making your home safer, more efficient,\nand more reliable — one water heater at a time.\n"},{"title":"10 Common Plumbing Myths Debunked by Experts","seoTitle":"Plumbing Myths vs Facts | Expert Analysis of Common Misconceptions","slug":"10-common-plumbing-myths-debunked-by-experts","date":"2025-05-12","lastModified":"2025-05-12","author":{"name":"Lisa Rodriguez","role":"Senior Plumbing Expert","image":{"src":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/1065084/pexels-photo-1065084.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1260&h=750&dpr=2","webp":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/1065084/pexels-photo-1065084.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1260&h=750&dpr=2","avif":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/1065084/pexels-photo-1065084.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1260&h=750&dpr=2","alt":"Lisa Rodriguez"}},"category":"Education","tags":["plumbing myths","fact checking","plumbing tips","homeowner education","professional advice"],"featuredImage":{"src":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/628241/pexels-photo-628241.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1260&h=750&dpr=2","webp":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/628241/pex